        </h3>
        <p>
          Multiple-choice question (MCQ) exams with four choices carry an inherent <strong>25% random guess probability</strong>. If an examination consisted of 100 questions with no penalty for wrong answers, an unprepared candidate blindly selecting random choices would statistically score approximately 25 correct answers simply by chance.
        </p>
        <p>
          To maintain fairness, meritocracy, and rigorous standard deviation, examination boards introduce negative marking. The primary objectives are:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
          <li><strong>Neutralizing Random Chance:</strong> By assigning a penalty that matches or exceeds the reciprocal of the number of distractors (e.g., 1/3 penalty for 4-option questions), random guessing yields an expected mathematical return of zero or negative points.</li>
          <li><strong>Rewarding Precision &amp; Conceptual Mastery:</strong> Competitive entrance exams test high-level accuracy. A candidate who scores 70 marks by attempting 75 questions is ranked substantially higher than a candidate who reaches 70 marks through 95 erratic attempts with numerous penalties.</li>
          <li><strong>Testing Risk Management Under Pressure:</strong> Modern civil service and engineering exams assess a candidate's judgment under tight time constraints—evaluating whether they can identify high-risk traps and make measured decisions.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

