        </h3>
        <p>
          Regardless of the testing authority, the net score calculation always relies on the balance between positive marks earned and penalty points forfeited:
        </p>

        <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50 p-4 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-950 font-mono text-xs sm:text-sm text-blue-900 dark:text-blue-300 overflow-x-auto space-y-1">
          <div><strong>Gross Score</strong> = Correct Answers &times; Marks Awarded per Question</div>
          <div><strong>Total Penalty</strong> = Incorrect Answers &times; Negative Deduction per Question</div>
          <div><strong>Net Score</strong> = Gross Score &minus; Total Penalty</div>
          <div><strong>Accuracy Percentage</strong> = (Correct Answers &divide; Total Attempted Questions) &times; 100</div>
        </div>

        <p>
          For example, in an examination where each question carries <strong>+2 marks</strong> with a <strong>1/3rd penalty</strong> (deduction of 0.667 marks per wrong answer), an aspirant who attempts 80 questions with 60 correct and 20 incorrect answers achieves:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-1 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
